60,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,"Option B is the most viable. It's on-site, meaning we can bill the regional fund tariffs, and it uses a dedicated space.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Shion,,Option A is the only one that mirrors the call-back trial's success. Reducing readmissions is the only metric that actually matters for the patients.,"{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Midori,,I cannot support A or B. The wards are too empty and therapy staff are leaving. Option C is the only one that fits our current staffing slack.,"{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Kenji,,"Option B and C both use ""scheduled"" slots, which the booking system can handle. But B needs more rooms, and we're already full by 11 AM!","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Shizue,,"Option D sounds lovely, but it requires coordination with providers outside the hospital. I fear the data agreements would take years to sign.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Midori, Option C is a nice sentiment, but does it generate revenue? We have a shrinking development budget. We need something that earns back.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Shion,,"@Jonathan, you're obsessed with the ledger. @Midori, if we use a dedicated team for Option A, we don't touch the ward nurses. The results are proven.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Jonathan"",""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Midori,,"@Shion, where does that ""dedicated team"" come from? We are already using agency cover for basic ward safety. We can't just invent new staff.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Shion, Option A is off-site, so it doesn't use rooms, but it's a nightmare for the booking system. It'll just be ""invisible work"" that disappears.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Shizue,,"@Midori, Option C is a joint effort between midwifery and pediatrics. We must be very sure they both agree to the budget and the rota in writing.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,Option C is on-site and uses available staff. It's more sustainable than A and less space-intensive than B. I can support C.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Shion,,"I find it absurd that we'd choose a ""support service"" over a program that prevents patients from returning to the hospital. I'm sticking with A.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Midori,,I feel much more comfortable with C. It protects our exhausted ward staff and uses the midwifery capacity we actually have. It's the most balanced choice.,"{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Kenji,,"If Option C uses the existing booking slots and doesn't need new parking or rooms, it's the only one that won't make the hospital seize up.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Shizue,,"If we can ensure a formal agreement between the two departments, Option C is a very gentle and sustainable way to grow. I agree.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
